lagfinder.memory.summary=%s counts  ·  objects %s → %s  ·  size %s → %s  ·  %s classes
lagfinder.memory.hint=Pick a class to see it here and on the graph. Pick a mod on the left to see only its classes.
lagfinder.tip.memory_sort=Sorting\nBy size: the bytes of the class at the last count.\nBy growth: how much it grew from the first count to the last. Growth alone is no proof of a leak (a cache filling up grows too), and objects created and collected in between are not seen.\nBy objects, by new objects: the same by the number of objects.
lagfinder.tip.memory_all_mods=Show the classes of all mods\nA class counts to the mod of its package (or jar): Java\'s and libraries\' classes count apart, even when a mod uses them. Not how much memory a mod holds.
lagfinder.tip.memory_pick_mod=Click to show only the classes of this mod
lagfinder.tip.memory_live=Before each count a full garbage collection is made, so only objects still in use are counted. Each count stops the game for a moment.
lagfinder.tip.memory_all=Objects are counted without a collection first, so garbage not yet collected is counted too.
lagfinder.tip.memory_count=Objects\nHow many objects of the class there were at the last count.
lagfinder.tip.memory_count_delta=Change of objects\nFrom the first count to the last: more (red) or fewer (green).
lagfinder.tip.memory_size=Size\nThe bytes of all objects of the class at the last count: the objects alone (shallow size), without what they point to. A mod\'s HashMap counts as Java\'s, though it holds the mod\'s data.
lagfinder.tip.memory_size_delta=Change of size\nFrom the first count to the last. Not a proof of a leak: a class that keeps growing scan after scan may be one; a heap dump shows what holds it.
lagfinder.tip.memory_trend=Trend\nThe size at each count, from the lowest to the highest.

